# <%= module %> — Nerves Gate app development

This workspace owns simulator configuration and tools. Each directory in `apps/`
is an independent, deployable OTP application, not an umbrella child. `my_app` is
created for you. Applications depend only on the SDK; simulator and generator
dependencies belong to this workspace and are never packaged with your apps.

```sh
mix setup                       # fetch dependencies and missing QEMU firmware
mix test                        # all app unit tests; no hardware starts
mix app.new telemetry           # create another independent app
mix setup                       # fetch dependencies for new apps
```

`mix setup` downloads a pinned **x86_64 QEMU firmware image** to
a versioned file under `firmware/` over HTTPS and verifies its SHA-256 checksum.
Existing managed images are verified before reuse. Updating the workspace tooling
and running setup selects its new pinned image; old and custom images are kept.
No GitHub CLI or firmware build is needed.

To use your own firmware, select its path in `config/simulator.exs` and provide
the file yourself. Missing custom paths are reported, never silently replaced
with the default image. Then:

```sh
mix sim                         # simulator site at http://localhost:4000
```

Linux, QEMU 8.2+ with user/stream networking, fwup, flock and mkfs.ext4 are
required. Setup checks prerequisites without starting devices. On Ubuntu/Debian:

```sh
sudo apt install git build-essential pkg-config libmnl-dev libnl-genl-3-dev qemu-system-x86 util-linux inotify-tools e2fsprogs
```

Install fwup from https://github.com/fwup-home/fwup/releases.
On Apple Silicon, use an arm64 Ubuntu VM (for example `orb create --arch arm64
ubuntu nerves-gate` with OrbStack). Clone and run inside its Linux filesystem;
avoid amd64/Rosetta. No product-repository checkout or firmware build is needed.
Generation and `mix setup` do not boot devices or format media.

Start the device on the site, open its Gateway UI and choose
**Application storage → Use this card**. The virtual SD card is already ext4;
using it preserves data. In another terminal in this workspace:

```sh
mix app.install my_app           # package, install and start on a running guest
mix app.install telemetry --device gateway_a
mix app.package telemetry       # packages/telemetry-1.0.0.ez
mix format                      # workspace and all apps
```

An app name is optional when only one app exists. Packaging refuses overwriting
an existing output; use `--output another.ez` or remove the old file explicitly.
Installing uses a temporary package and preserves application data. Stop the
simulator with Ctrl+C in its original terminal. Reset erases disposable device
disks, SD cards and enrollment. Manual controls operate only on owned QEMU guests.

Each app can also be developed independently: `cd apps/my_app && mix test`.
The generated example makes decisions from SDK structs using pure functions;
its tests need neither a configured backend nor fake hardware. Use SDK calls in
supervised workers and keep decisions separate from effects. Use QEMU to verify
integration with real firmware. These are cold app installs, not hot-code reloads.
For device diagnostics, use an authorized SSH IEx console and `RingLogger.attach()`;
never put credentials into application logs.
For deployment to a real device, commission its SD card and install the `.ez`
archive through its administrative application API. Apps are trusted code in the
firmware BEAM, not a sandbox; use only the public SDK, and handle missing storage.
Native dependencies must be built for the device architecture.
